<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tumbuka people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tumbuka are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and Tanzania. Living in small farming communities, they came under the loose authority of the Chikulamayembe dynasty in the late 18th century and in the mid-19th century were conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbelwa">Mbelwa</a>. Their language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbuka-language">Chitumbuka</a>, is widely spoken across northern Malawi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tumbuka-people.jpg" alt="Tumbuka people" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tumbuka Arch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Gonapamuhanya_festival_2025.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Like several neighbouring peoples, the Tumbuka trace their origins to the Luba Kingdom in what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, which they are said to have left in the early 1400s. Oral history holds that they travelled with the ancestors of the Hehe and Gogo peoples of Tanzania and the Mwakyusa under an overall leader named Mudala, the Tumbuka themselves being led by Mulonga Mbulalubilo. They probably entered the land between the Luangwa valley and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> in the fifteenth century. More than fifteen related groups, among them the Senga, Nyika, Henga, Yombe, Phoka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>, speak dialects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbuka-language">Chitumbuka</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the start of the eighteenth century the Tumbuka lived in small, independent communities without any central organisation. Traders from the Unyamwezi region of present-day Tanzania, who came for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and to some extent slaves, allied with groups of Henga, and their leader founded the Chikulamayembe dynasty, which ruled a federation of small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>; by the 1830s it was in decline. In the 1840s Jumbe Salim bin Abdallah set up a trading centre at Nkhotakota, and his trade in slaves for the clove plantations of Zanzibar brought raids and violence against the Tumbuka.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbelwa">Mbelwa</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a>, settled in what is now Mzimba district around 1855. They treated the Henga as subjects, exacting tribute and taking captives who were kept as unfree agricultural workers or enrolled in Ngoni regiments. Under colonial rule, from the 1880s, Tumbuka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> men left to work as porters and estate labourers in the south of Nyasaland, and mission-educated Tumbuka went on to Southern Rhodesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Levi Mumba, Charles Chinula and many other leaders of the movement that won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>'s independence in 1964 were Tumbuka-speaking northerners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Tumbuka society is patrilineal: on marriage a woman moves to her husband's village, a custom generally traced to Mulowoka's settlement and still more to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a> invasion. A child's first name is given by the father and paternal grandfather once the umbilical cord has detached. Traditional religion centres on a supreme creator, Chiuta, associated with the sun, alongside reverence for ancestor spirits. Most Tumbuka today are Christian. Vimbuza, therapeutic drumming, music and dance for illness held to come from possessing spirits, belongs to the wider ngoma complex of central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Malawi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of September 2018 counted 1,614,955 Tumbuka, 9.2 percent of the population.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tumbuka people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Tumbuka</b> tradition recalls that in the late 18th century traders known as the Balowoka crossed Lake Malawi from the east seeking ivory.</blockquote><a href="/state/chikulamayembe-state">Chikulamayembe State</a></li><li><blockquote>Captives from the <b>Tumbuka</b>, Tonga and Chewa were absorbed, so that the Ngoni language gave way to Chitumbuka.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-ngoni-kingdom">Northern Ngoni Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
